.\" man/man3/Docv::Theme.3cr
.\" Generated by docv gen-cr from README.md and the doc comments; regenerate instead of editing.
.TH "Docv::Theme" "3cr" "2026-11-03" "docv 0.1.0" "docv API Reference"
.SH "NAME"
Docv::Theme \- The viewer\(aqs styles by role.
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.nf
\fBrequire "docv"\fR
.sp
\fBclass Docv::Theme\fR
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
The viewer\(aqs styles by role. A role\(aqs spec is a list of words:
attributes (\fB\%bold\fR, \fB\%italic\fR, \fB\%underline\fR, \fB\%reverse\fR, \fB\%dim\fR), a
foreground color, and \fB\%on\fR followed by a background color, e.g.
\fB\%bold \%cyan\fR, \fB\%black \%on \%yellow\fR, \fB\%on \%background+12\fR.
.PP
A color is \fB\%default\fR, \fB\%foreground\fR, \fB\%background\fR, an ANSI name (\fB\%black\fR,
\fB\%red\fR, \fB\%green\fR, \fB\%yellow\fR, \fB\%blue\fR, \fB\%magenta\fR, \fB\%cyan\fR, \fB\%white\fR, and
\fB\%bright\-\fR before any of them), a palette number \fB\%0\fR\-\fB\%255\fR or \fB\%#rrggbb\fR.
ANSI names and numbers are the terminal theme\(aqs own colors. A \fB\%+N\fR or
\fB\%\-N\fR suffix ramps a color N percent toward the theme\(aqs foreground or
background, so \fB\%background+12\fR is a shade off the background in a dark
and a light theme alike.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
theme = Docv::Theme.new({Docv::Theme::Role::Heading1 => Docv::Theme.parse("bold #ff8800")})
ids = theme.styles(Docv::Palette.new, Docv::ColorDepth::TrueColor)
Docv::Styles[ids[Docv::Theme::Role::Heading1.value]] # => bold, fg #ff8800
.EE
.RE
.SH "CONSTANTS"
.TP 4
\fBATTRIBUTES\fR
.RS
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
ATTRIBUTES = {
  "bold" => Attr::Bold,
  "italic" => Attr::Italic,
  "underline" => Attr::Underline,
  "reverse" => Attr::Reverse,
  "dim" => Attr::Dim,
}
.EE
.RE
.RE
.TP 4
\fBCOLORS\fR
.RS
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
COLORS = begin
  names = {"default" => Paint.new, "foreground" => Paint.new(Base::Foreground), "background" => Paint.new(Base::Background)}
  (["black", "red", "green", "yellow", "blue", "magenta", "cyan", "white"] of ::String).each_with_index do |name, index|
    names[name] = Paint.new(Base::Fixed, Color.palette(index))
    names["bright\-#{name}"] = Paint.new(Base::Fixed, Color.palette(index + 8))
  end
  names
end
.EE
.RE
.PP
Color names: the bases, then the ANSI colors and their bright variants.
.RE
.TP 4
\fBDEFAULT\fR
.RS
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
